{"parentUuid":"c57720c4-9376-4ece-aec5-95792e4aceb2","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ace34c9d71e0db288","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CffXaQ2mtXV9Pq9gogTmq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both pending cells of `stop-block-cap` are now unsupported with evidence from recorded runs, and PR #96 is merged. All CI checks passed and `sr-checks` printed no refusals. Because neither cell is supported, there is no adapter code, markers or tests. The only changes are the two runs and the two cells in `spec/capabilities/stop-block-cap.yaml`.\n\n- **codex: unsupported.** A new run, `codex-mock/snapshots/runs/stop-block-cap-30`, has a Stop hook that blocks on its first 30 calls, then lets the turn end. Real Codex continued the turn after every one of those 30 blocks and fired Stop a 31st time. It never overrode a block, so there is no cap. The cell cites that run and the already-frozen Codex hooks doc (`#stop`).\n- **cursor: unsupported.** A new run, `cursor-mock/snapshots/runs/stop-block-cap-always`, configures `stop`, `afterAgentResponse`, `sessionStart` and `sessionEnd` hooks and makes a tool call. In real `cursor-agent -p` only `sessionStart` and `sessionEnd` fired. `stop` never did, so nothing can block the turn and no cap applies. Cursor's hooks doc does describe a `loop_limit` (default 5) for stop hooks. I cited only the run, not the doc, and went with the recording over the doc.\n\nMy first attempt on Codex, a hook that blocks forever, never ended and would not seal, which is why the Codex run stops blocking after 30.\n\nNothing is left red."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":174,"cache_read_input_tokens":126762,"output_tokens":531,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tok